    id: "family",
    title: "Family",
    blurb: "Talk about your family and their jobs, join words with ও, আর and কিন্তু, and make people plural with -রা.",
    deck: "Family and people",
    body: `
<p>Family words are short and easy in Bengali. The two things that work differently from English are the word for "and" and the way you say "more than one".</p>
<h3>The core words</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>English</th><th>Bengali</th></tr><tr><td>mother</td><td class="k">মা</td></tr><tr><td>father</td><td class="k">বাবা</td></tr><tr><td>brother</td><td class="k">ভাই</td></tr><tr><td>sister</td><td class="k">বোন</td></tr><tr><td>son, boy</td><td class="k">ছেলে</td></tr><tr><td>daughter, girl</td><td class="k">মেয়ে</td></tr></table></div>
<p>In Bangladesh you will also hear আব্বা <i>abba</i> and আম্মা <i>amma</i> for father and mother at home; মা and বাবা are understood everywhere. Jobs follow the zero copula you already know: no verb for "is". Add আমার, তোমার or আপনার in front, exactly as with any other noun.</p>
{{ex:আমার বাবা ডাক্তার।|amar baba ḍaktar.|My father is a doctor.}}
{{ex:সে আমার ভাই।|she amar bhai.|He is my brother.}}
<h3>And, and, but</h3>
<p>Bengali has two everyday words for "and". <b>ও</b> <i>o</i> and <b>আর</b> <i>ar</i> both join two nouns. <b>কিন্তু</b> <i>kintu</i> is "but". ও can also mean "also", and আর can also mean "more", so listen to the whole sentence. For joining two family words, either one is fine. Put কিন্তু between two complete sentences, after a comma, the way English uses "but".</p>
{{ex:আমার মা ও বাবা|amar ma o baba|my mother and father}}
{{ex:আমার একটা বোন আছে, কিন্তু ভাই নেই।|amar ækṭa bon achhe, kintu bhai nei.|I have a sister, but no brother.}}
<h3>More than one person: -রা</h3>
<p>English adds -s. Bengali joins the plural classifier -রা to a word for people: <b>ছেলেরা</b> <i>chhelera</i> "the boys", <b>মেয়েরা</b> <i>meyera</i> "the girls". After a consonant it is usually -েরা: <b>বোনেরা</b> <i>bonera</i>.</p>
{{ex:আমার বন্ধুরা ঢাকায় থাকে।|amar bondhura ḍhakay thake.|My friends live in Dhaka.}}
{{ex:ছেলেরা আর মেয়েরা|chhelera ar meyera|the boys and the girls}}
<h3>Numbers win</h3>
<p>When a number is there, the number does the counting, so the noun takes no -রা. Use the people classifier -জন from the numbers lesson.</p>
{{ex:আমার দুইজন ভাই আছে।|amar duijon bhai achhe.|I have two brothers.}}
<p>The classifier -জন belongs to the number, and the noun after it stays plain. That is the same rule you saw with দুইটা বই. So -রা and a number never meet on the same noun: choose one or the other.</p>
<p>Elders often get the polite verb ending when you talk about them: আমার মা ঢাকায় থাকেন. That is respect, not plural.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> thinking of English -s and adding -রা even after a number. দুইজন বন্ধুরা → <b>দুইজন বন্ধু</b>.</div>
`,
